/* Found documents. Keep puzzle directions explicit; uncertainty belongs to the story. */
static const char *const ht_evidence_prose[HT_LEVELS*3] = {
    "The cloth is caught on the sheltered side of the trunk, where rain cannot reach it. I recognise the uneven hem. She sewed it during the winter when our roof leaked, pretending not to notice that I was watching her hands shake.\n\nUnderneath is an older cut: three narrow grooves, black with sap. Her thread passes through the first. A stranger would see a repair. We used to leave messages in repairs.\n\nOn the reverse she has stitched: \"Begin with the highest bell. Wait for it to finish. The wood carries sound farther than the air.\"\n\nThe bark has grown around the grooves. Whoever first marked this path is older than our disappearance, older perhaps than the fog. Did she follow their instructions too? I put the cloth back where another traveller might find it and copy the stitch into this book.",
    "The tin smells of wet flour. Inside, the mill register has been folded so that the column headed DELIVERIES faces outward. There are no weights or prices. Only names, and little squares where a clerk should have written the quantities.\n\nAn older hand explains: \"One square means arrived. Two means crossed. Leave the last open until the far bell answers. After the high note, sound the low one.\"\n\nNear the bottom I find my sister's handwriting. Beneath her own name she has added six others. Their ink is paler, as though the pen was running dry. None has a final square.\n\nI used to imagine a trail as something one person left for another. This page makes it look more like a room, with people arriving, waiting, then vanishing through a door. I cannot tell whether the empty squares mean danger or simply that nobody stayed behind to fill them.",
    "It is not a bell at all, but a piece of wood carved into the shape of three bells. A nail is tied to it on a string. Someone has struck each shape often enough to polish the grain, although all three would make the same small sound.\n\nUnder the middle bell is a deep notch. On the back, in letters that slope down the wood: \"Last is the one in between. Then they let us through.\"\n\nThere is a taller figure beside six small scratches. The tallest figure has a scarf, but no face. I wonder whether the child could not remember it or simply ran out of space.\n\nMy sister once told me that a toy is a way to carry home when your hands are full. This one was left carefully above the mud. Was it a gift to the next child, or a record that the crossing had worked?",
    "The drawing is on the back of a classroom exercise. Above the rooftops, a child has copied the same name six times. Each attempt is smaller, as if the name might fit somewhere if written carefully enough.\n\nMy sister has added the roof route in charcoal. Ladders hang between buildings which no longer share a street. At the first relay she has shaded the left window and written: \"Keep this one ON. The light must reach the next roof.\"\n\nAlong the margin is another instruction: \"If a door opens toward you, tie it back. The person behind may have only one free hand.\"\n\nI remember how angry I was when she left without waking me. Now I picture her crossing these roofs, stopping at every door, building a path for someone she could not see. It does not answer why she went. It makes the question harder to ask.",
    "The fuse is black from end to end, wrapped in a label cut from a school notebook. The wire inside has melted into a bead. Somebody kept the useless thing after fitting its replacement.\n\n\"Middle relay OFF,\" reads the label. \"It shorts across the return. Do not bridge it because the room looks dark.\"\n\nBelow, my sister has written an account of three attempts. The first produced smoke. The second lit one floor for a breath. The third entry contains only a time and a small drawing of a window.\n\nAnother hand has added: \"We saw it.\"\n\nThe beacon was here before she was. So were the faults, and somebody who knew how to name them. I had mistaken every light for a private message. This little burnt wire suggests that a message can pass through many hands without belonging any less to the person who sent it.",
    "The watch log is nailed behind a roof vent. Its pages have swollen into a fan, but the names are legible where sleeves sheltered the ink. No one has signed two consecutive watches.\n\n\"Right relay ON. Both outer windows lit; centre dark. Do not change the arrangement to signal a friend. It marks a safe roof for everyone.\"\n\nThe entries record weather, fuel and things briefly mistaken for people. A coat on an aerial. A moving sheet. A light beneath the fog which did not appear again.\n\nMy sister's final entry says: \"West tomorrow. Still trying.\" Underneath, another watcher has written: \"We will keep looking from here.\"\n\nI have been following her absence as though it were a single empty space. In this log it has edges: a watch someone took over, a cup someone washed, a lamp someone refilled after she had gone.",
    "A metal tag hangs from the service lantern. The stamped number has been rubbed almost flat by a thumb. Its paper ration strip is newer, folded twice around the handle to keep it out of the flame.\n\n\"First valve: ONE measure for the lamps. More only smokes the glass. Save the light, not the flame.\"\n\nSeven allowances follow. My sister's is crossed out. Beside the smallest three she has written, \"Give them mine until morning.\"\n\nOn the reverse is a child's question: \"Does the dark use fuel too?\" There is no written answer. Below the question, someone has drawn a sun so large that it runs off the paper.\n\nI once thought bravery meant not being afraid of the dark. I can see her here now, counting the oil and pretending that one measure was plenty, while a child watched her face for an answer.",
    "The stove plate comes loose in my hand. Behind it is a scrap protected from both weather and fire. A mechanic has drawn three strokes beneath the second valve.\n\n\"THREE measures to the heaters in a cold watch. Open the flue first. If the little ones sleep, let them.\"\n\nThe rest is a list of things heard during the storm: pumps turning without power, stones striking the roof, someone knocking from under the floor. The last line is in my sister's hand: \"A pipe, loose at the bracket. We tied it.\"\n\nBelow that, much smaller: \"Still sounded like knocking.\"\n\nThe soot holds three handprints at different heights. One has an adult outline around it, made by wiping rather than touching. I cannot decide whether they were measuring the children or making a picture. Either way, someone stopped counting fuel long enough to play.",
    "The can is empty but tightly stoppered. A rolled list rattles inside. The first line is practical: \"Third valve: TWO for the road. Leave the reserve sealed until the next station.\"\n\nBelow it, names are paired with tasks. Carry water. Watch the rear. Count at each crossing. Beside the oldest traveller: \"Tell us when to stop. This is a task too.\"\n\nMy sister has ruled an eighth line beneath the seven names. It has no task and no tick against it. I know before I unfold the bottom what she intended to write there.\n\nThe empty line frightens me more than a farewell would. She allowed for my arrival while she was running out of things to share. I copy the list without filling the space. For now, the book can hold the place she kept for me.",
    "The ticket is dated, but the year has washed away. Its destination survives: HOME. Under it a clerk has stamped VALID UNTIL USED. The railway must once have believed that was a promise it could keep.\n\nOn the reverse, a later traveller has crossed out the left track. \"Cutting down. Left signal OFF. No train can reach us this way.\"\n\nA second note reads: \"We waited until the frost reached the carriage steps. She said waiting was also a decision. We walked at first light.\"\n\nThere are two sets of pinholes in the ticket. It was displayed somewhere, taken down, then displayed again. I imagine each family reading it and deciding whether home meant a place ahead or a place to return to. Someone finally left the ticket here rather than carry that decision any farther.",
    "The map has been corrected so often that the rails resemble roots. A switchman's neat blue lines end at a bridge. A rough pencil path continues beyond the printed edge.\n\n\"Middle signal ON. It alone reaches the bridge. All other routes closed. Test the points before moving the children.\"\n\nMy sister has written a question in the margin: \"Will you come?\" The reply is folded under the map: \"Not on this crossing. Someone must leave the points set.\"\n\nNo name follows. Only the drawing of a lamp with its shutter open.\n\nI search the little hut for some sign of what happened afterward, but find only a cup turned upside down and a coat hook worn bright. The map gives me a route. It cannot tell me whether the person who drew it ever used it. I have begun to understand why she left so many questions unanswered.",
    "The envelope is tucked beneath a carriage seat, safely above the waterline. Instead of an address, it bears a drawing of a lamp. The letter inside uses almost the whole page for four words: \"THANK YOU LAMP KEEPER.\"\n\nA careful adult has added: \"Right signal OFF. Tunnel fallen beyond the bend. We have taken the middle route.\"\n\nThere is more on the flap, where the child has pressed too hard: \"We left your blanket dry. We did not take the cup. Please come before the snow gets inside.\"\n\nI know this is not a letter to me, though for a moment I want it to be. It was left for a person who might arrive after everyone else had gone. I put it back under the seat. A book may keep a copy, but it should not steal the chance of an answer.",
    "The ferry token is tied beneath a board with fishing line. Its edge is worn thin enough to cut a finger. One side shows a boat; the other shows the same three grooves I found in the forest.\n\nA note says: \"Begin at the near LEFT bell. Call before you untie. Sound carries across water when lamps cannot.\"\n\nBelow, a list records what the fare may be paid with: fuel, bread, repair, news. The last item is crossed out and replaced with \"nothing, if nothing is what you have.\"\n\nThe token cannot have paid for seven people, yet seven sets of marks pass across the board. Perhaps it was never money. Perhaps it was handed back each time so another stranger would have something to offer. I leave it tied beneath the board, where the next hand will reach.",
    "Two knots keep the paper above the highest stain of water. The first is a working knot. The second is decorative, a little unnecessary loop made by someone with time to wait.\n\n\"After near LEFT, call far RIGHT, then MIDDLE. Do not pull until the answering rope takes the weight.\"\n\nThe boatman has listed the crossings by loads rather than names: children and bedding; tools and food; those who could pull; those who could not. Beside the last load my sister has added a second line of rope in ink.\n\n\"He would not leave the chair,\" she writes. \"So we brought the chair.\"\n\nA small kindness, until I look at the current. Each extra object meant another risk, another pull against the water. The empty ferry house is full of decisions I cannot see. The decorative knot holds one of them above the flood.",
    "Water has taken the lower half of every line. I read the log by holding it sideways, finding the pressure marks where the ink has gone.\n\n\"Finish where you began: near LEFT.\" The boatman repeated this at the top of each page, perhaps for an assistant, perhaps because tired people forget simple things.\n\nMy sister and six passengers appear together. One name begins confidently and dissolves into a trembling line. Under it she has written: \"Waited. Crossed when he could stand. All accounted for.\"\n\nThe waiting occupies two dates. There is no complaint, only a note about extra blankets.\n\nI have measured her journey by the distance between signs. This page contains another kind of distance: two days spent going nowhere because one person needed them. I close the log more carefully than I opened it.",
    "The pouch holds three white stones and a dark button. A face is scratched into one stone: two pits and a mouth that changes expression when turned upside down.\n\nWritten inside the cloth: \"First drum lifts. THREE turns on the setting. Keep the little stones out of the gears.\"\n\nOn the outside, a child has labelled the stones Mother, Brother and Me. The button has no name. It is secured by a loop so it cannot fall out.\n\nI remember travelling with my sister when we were small. She would name the hills to make the road shorter, then forget the names and let me correct her. Here someone has made a family light enough to carry in a pocket. The real people left no explanation for the button. I turn it over, looking for a fourth name, and find only the worn remains of thread.",
    "The brake notice has been patched with cloth. A strip of my sister's scarf binds the repaired handle, where bare wire would have cut a palm.\n\n\"Middle drum holds at ONE. Set the brake before crossing. Do not use the lift count on the brake.\"\n\nThe printed warning beneath it says that passengers are forbidden. Someone has pencilled seven little figures across the word.\n\nOn the back is a repair account: two bolts borrowed from the fence, a spring taken from a door, cloth from a traveller. Nobody asks for repayment. Instead, at the bottom: \"Replace what fails for whoever comes next.\"\n\nThere is no signature. I would have recognised her repair even without the scarf. She always left a smooth place for a hand to rest. I test the binding before I move on, and tighten the knot where the rain has loosened it.",
    "The note has been torn away from something longer. Only the lower corner remains beneath a stone.\n\n\"Last drum returns the hoist at TWO. Leave it ready on this side. Three children. Two adults. One old man. Everyone is still with me.\"\n\nFor the first time the six names have bodies I can picture. The count does not include her. She has made herself the person doing the counting.\n\nBelow, in a different hand: \"Seven, including you.\"\n\nThe correction is underlined twice. I sit down to read it again. Whoever wrote it saw something I know too well: her habit of making herself disappear inside the work that needed doing. I had been afraid she was lost. Someone on this road was afraid of a different kind of disappearance, and took the trouble to count her back in.",
    "The packet is empty, but its seams have been opened carefully so that not even one seed would be trapped inside. The printed picture shows a plant taller than a person. Beside it, somebody has drawn the much smaller result.\n\n\"Left bed failed. Shutter OFF. Save its light for the living rows. Do not mistake an empty bed for an empty store.\"\n\nA note in my sister's hand records which seeds were planted and which were kept dry. Most of the page is given to the failures. At the bottom she has written: \"Enough held back to try again.\"\n\nI had thought these glasshouses were a place they abandoned. The packet suggests a less final word. A bed may be empty because something died, or because the gardener has decided to wait. Without the notes, the two would look exactly the same.",
    "A garden label has been used until there is no blank surface left. The front names the crop. The back counts sprouts in groups of five. Around the edge a child has made room for one last mark.\n\n\"Middle bed: light ON. Keep the shutter open. Turn the trays when the stems lean.\"\n\nI recognise the heavy downward strokes from the letter in the train. Here the letters are steadier. The child has learnt to write \"tomorrow\" and uses it three times.\n\nThere is an argument in the margin. One hand says they must leave before the frost. Another says the seedlings cannot travel. My sister has drawn a box around both and written, \"Then we find someone who can stay.\"\n\nThe last sprout count is in a new hand. It is an answer, though no one has written yes.",
    "The drawing shows a lantern larger than the person carrying it. My sister is recognisable by her scarf, reduced now to a few short strokes. Behind her stand six figures. Ahead of her is an empty outline with room for another.\n\n\"Right bed: light ON too,\" says a practical note along the bottom. \"The two living beds share the evening watch.\"\n\nAcross the lantern, in the child's heavy letters: \"WE WILL KEEP IT LIT.\"\n\nI thought she was leading these people toward a place she already knew. The drawing gives her no map, only a lamp. Perhaps that was all they needed to believe she knew the way.\n\nA second wick is sketched inside the flame. It might be a mistake. It might be somebody explaining that the promise no longer rests on one pair of hands. I copy it exactly, including the empty outline.",
    "The gauge has cracked in a curve like a shoreline. Through the glass I can still read the instruction: \"First regulator: TWO to the turbine. Listen before opening further.\"\n\nThe repair log begins in my sister's hand. It describes the sound before power returned: a dragging scrape, a pause, then the same scrape again. She wrote that it sounded like someone trying to say a word through sleep.\n\nHalfway down, the handwriting changes. The new mechanic lists the fault, the parts and the load. On the next page somebody has drawn a lamp and written only, \"It stayed on.\"\n\nShe did not finish this repair alone. I trace the place where one hand gives way to another. It is the clearest sign of progress I have found, and it is almost nothing: the same work, continued by someone who had learned enough to take over.",
    "The pipe runs away from the ridge, back toward the gardens. Its direction startled me; I have become so used to looking only ahead.\n\n\"Middle regulator: ONE measure. Garden return. Do not shut this to brighten the tower. A light is no use to people with nothing to eat.\"\n\nA maintenance card lists the buried joints. Beside each, a space for the next inspection. These are not the notes of people fleeing as fast as they can. They expect to come back, or expect someone else to care enough to follow the pipe.\n\nMy sister has written one question: \"Who is here when we are not?\" Beneath it are three names and a rota.\n\nFor weeks I have wanted the trail to end. Now I wonder whether its purpose was ever an ending. The water is travelling in the opposite direction, quietly keeping a place alive behind us.",
    "The circuit diagram is pinned beneath a list of watches. Its lines connect the dam to a point above the map, where the draughtsman has written RIDGE rather than drawing the mountain.\n\n\"Last regulator: THREE to the ridge circuit. Leave the return connected. Never trade the lower lamps for the upper signal.\"\n\nThere are many names on the rota. Some I recognise from the mill; others appear nowhere else. My sister has signed fewer watches than I expected.\n\nAt the bottom is a rule: \"When a watcher leaves, write who takes their place. A blank is not permission to turn it off.\"\n\nI had imagined her alone at the top of the mountain, holding a light out for me until her arms failed. This page offers something more durable and less simple. People I have never met have been taking turns with that weight.",
    "The remaining scarf is shorter than I remember. Along one edge are the places where she cut strips: not ragged tears, but small deliberate incisions, each finished so the whole would not unravel.\n\nThe first stitched mark sits beneath a drawing of the MIDDLE chime. It matches the pattern in the forest, though the route here begins on a different note.\n\nInside the fold is a line I nearly miss: \"I did not know which signs would survive. I left more than one.\"\n\nI have accused the trail of being broken whenever I lost it. She knew it would break. The cloth, the notes, the lamps and the people were all different answers to that knowledge.\n\nI hold the two ends together in my mind. There is not enough scarf left to keep anyone warm. She turned it into something that might bring warmth to a person who had not arrived yet.",
    "Under the shelter stone the wind is suddenly silent. The message is scratched into its underside, so the person who wrote it had to lift the weight before making each mark.\n\n\"After MIDDLE, sound low LEFT, then high RIGHT. Wait through the echo. The mountain repeats what it hears.\"\n\nBelow the instructions: \"I kept a light for you every night. Some nights I could only keep it in my head. I hope those count.\"\n\nThere is no promise that she knew I was coming. That is what makes me stay beneath the stone long after I have read it. She kept a place for uncertainty instead of pretending it was certainty.\n\nWhen I lower the slab, I turn it just enough to shelter the letters from the rain. The message was meant for me. The dry place beneath it can still be useful to someone else.",
    "One side of the tin is polished, the other marked with soot. It makes a poor mirror. My face breaks around dents and bends, but a distant light stays whole when I angle it toward the pass.\n\n\"Return to MIDDLE for the last chime. Then look beyond the notch. Do not answer your own reflection.\"\n\nThe instruction is followed by a series of dates and weather marks. Cloud. Snow. Clear for a moment. Beside one clear interval, my sister has written, \"Thought I saw movement below. Could not be sure.\"\n\nI cannot place myself on that date. I may have been asleep, or standing on some lower path, looking up. We could have been watching each other without knowing.\n\nThe polished side is worn where a thumb steadied it. I hold it the same way, and wait until a light appears that does not move with my hand.",
    "The lamp has been repaired with pieces that do not match. Its shutter is new; its stand is older than the settlement. A little arrow points toward it from the rail.\n\n\"Use the FIRST lamp, the one on the LEFT,\" says the label. \"The others face the lower paths. Leave their watches undisturbed.\"\n\nThe arrow is drawn in the same heavy hand as the garden promise. Beside it stand three small figures with boxes at their feet. The children have drawn themselves unpacking.\n\nI had wanted proof of arrival to look like a door opening. This is quieter: belongings taken out of bundles, a lamp given a permanent stand, a drawing made by someone who expects to leave it on a wall.\n\nThere is an empty hook beside the three coats. No name. I touch it once, then put my hand back on the rail.",
    "The radio card is soft from being passed between watches. The top instruction reads: \"THREE short flashes. Release between each. Keep to the first lamp.\"\n\nBelow it is our childhood signal, written in a careful hand that is not hers. Beside that, a pronunciation guide for my name. Someone has crossed out the wrong stress and written it again.\n\nThe evening calls fill both sides. Weather, strength, reply. Most reply boxes are empty. One says \"static only\"; another says \"voice, too faint.\" None says stop.\n\nI used to think hearing my name would prove that I had found her. Now I see how many strangers practised saying it before I could hear. She entrusted the sound of home to people I had never met. They kept calling, even when all the card offered them was another empty box.",
    "The note is folded beneath a cup at the last watch. My sister has used ordinary paper. After so many scraps rescued from machinery, its clean edges seem almost extravagant.\n\n\"Same first lamp for all three flashes. Left, left, left. Let the shutter close between them. I will know.\"\n\nThen, beneath a long space: \"There is a place for you here. I don't know whether you will find this. I am trying to learn that keeping a place is different from standing still.\"\n\nShe lists small things: a dry blanket, a cup without a crack, a window that opens toward the morning. No explanation for the fog. No claim that the road has become safe. Just things a person can keep ready.\n\nAt the bottom: \"If someone is with you, bring them too.\"\n\nI close the journal. For the first time, the next part does not need to be read from something she left behind.",
};

/* Reflections unlock alongside their matching narration beat, never ahead of it. */
static const char *const ht_story_prose[HT_LEVELS][3] = {
    {"Three flashes crossed the fog last night. My sister taught me that signal when we were children, before we had anything worth hiding. I have set out with a book because memory changes things when it is frightened. I want to keep a record of what I actually find.", "She left to find a way out of the valley. Cloth on the branches resembles her scarf, but a resemblance is not an answer. Every bend gives me somewhere new to hope, and somewhere new to be wrong.", "At the old mill, her scarf is tied where a traveller would see it before the bells. Farther on, a light blinks above the city. I cannot yet tell whether it is calling to me or warning everyone away."},
    {"The streets have disappeared beneath the fog. Her chalk arrows cross the roofs instead. Up here the buildings are separate islands; the city I remember survives only in the gaps between them.", "A lowered ladder, a door tied open: signs of someone expecting another person to follow. I have been looking for words, but work can leave a message too.", "The beacon is a lamp attached to a timer. It can keep calling after its maker has gone. Her map points west and says she is still trying. I will have to follow something less certain than a light."},
    {"Cactus has broken through the service roads. The derricks stand over the sand like machinery waiting for an instruction nobody remembers. Each has a path worn toward it; none is moving.", "Her empty fuel can makes the weight of this journey real. A lamp is easy to imagine from a distance. Someone has to carry what keeps it lit.", "The station radio clicks but gives me no voice. A note follows the railway out of the field. I leave with the impression of having arrived just after a conversation ended."},
    {"No train has crossed the yard in years. Her footprints pass between the tracks, using the railway for a purpose its makers never intended. The rails still point somewhere even when they can no longer take anyone there.", "Water and a blanket wait inside a carriage. I want to believe she left them for me. I cannot prove it; perhaps a stranger will need them more. I take only enough to go on.", "The signal wires reach a flooded bridge and stop. A bell moves across the water. Beyond the last reliable wire, people must have found another way to answer one another."},
    {"Posts and strips of cloth mark a crossing through the water. Everything else has lost its edges. I go slowly, testing each place where someone once decided a foot could be trusted.", "The ferry-house bell is tied against the wind. Its silence is deliberate. Whatever pattern calls the crossing must be different from the random ringing a storm would make.", "Her note mentions voices upstream. The trail rises toward the quarry. For the first time I am following a possibility that she did not simply leave people behind."},
    {"Dust fills the quarry cuts until depth becomes impossible to judge. Her marks show best on the darkest stone. I begin to look for what has been placed carefully, rather than what is brightest.", "Small footprints accompany hers. She was travelling with others. The marks I took for hesitation may have been the pace of someone shorter, older or more tired.", "Three stones form an arrow toward the glasshouses. A group has passed here and chosen to leave the route legible. Their crossing is over; mine still depends on it."},
    {"Someone planted beneath these broken roofs after the vines had already claimed them. The new rows interrupt the ruin. I had not expected to find evidence of a future here.", "A child's drawing shows my sister carrying a lantern. She led people to shelter when the cold came. I know her face in the picture even though the child has drawn almost none of it.", "They went toward the dam before winter. The beds look empty, but seeds remain. I cannot call this place abandoned while something has been set aside for another season."},
    {"The reservoir resembles a white sea. Below it, the spillways lead into machinery large enough to make a person seem incidental. Yet someone has kept a path through it.", "A repaired generator supplies the ridge. Her signal needed more than courage: parts, water, labour, and somebody willing to stay with the machine when others climbed ahead.", "My name is written on the door. The instruction beneath it is to keep climbing. After all the ambiguous marks, these words are meant for exactly one reader."},
    {"Bare trees lean away from the wind. The signal mast appears and vanishes between peaks. I have followed signs for so long that seeing their destination feels less solid than seeing another arrow.", "The remaining scarf lies where the paths divide. So little of it is left. Each earlier piece was a chance she was willing to spend without knowing whether I would find it.", "Three flashes reach me through the clouds. This light holds. I wait through another sequence, and another, afraid of moving too quickly and losing the certainty I have just been given."},
    {"There are roofs beyond the ridge, and smoke above the chimneys. The windows are lit. This is a place people have chosen to keep habitable, not merely a destination on a map.", "I turn the watchtower radio and hear my sister say my name through the static. All the pages in this book have been words left behind. This voice is happening now.", "I answer with three short flashes. She is waiting. The trail has brought me almost home, but it has also taught me to look behind before closing a door. Whoever follows should find the way open."},
};
